course: dictionary
lesson: 6689
title: Obviously: clear, of course, or cross
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Obviously, we'll need coats if it snows.» Here obviously means clearly, so anyone can see the answer. Check it by swapping in clearly: "Clearly, we'll need coats if it snows." It still makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
explain: «She was obviously scared.» The word tells you that her fear was easy to see, even if she did not say a word. Look at the words near obviously for clues. Words like everyone, surely and clearly often sit close by.
explain: Obviously can also mean "of course", as in «Obviously, I said yes!» The tone of voice matters too. If someone says "Obviously!" in a cross way, they mean you should have known already, which is sarcasm.
fact: Obviously is an adverb, so it tells you how something is seen, known or done.
fact: «Obviously» means in a way that is easy to see or understand; clearly.
fact: «Obviously» can also mean "of course" or "as you know", as in «Obviously, I said yes!»
fact: Said in a cross or sarcastic way, «Obviously!» means the thing should have been clear already.
fact: Phrases you may meet include «obviously not» and «quite obviously».
exercise: «She was obviously scared.» What does obviously mean here? | Very loudly | Slowly and softly | Secretly, so nobody could tell | *Clearly, so anyone could see she was scared
exercise: «Obviously, I said yes!» What does obviously mean here? | Not at all | In a hidden way | *Of course, as you know | Sadly, after a long time
exercise: Mum rolls her eyes and says «Obviously!» when you ask where your shoes are. What does she mean? | *It should have been clear already, so she is cross | She has lost her own shoes | She is pleased you asked a good question | She is whispering a secret
test: Which sentence uses obviously to mean "of course"? | «The lake was obviously frozen.» | «The dog was obviously hungry.» | «The room was obviously tidy.» | *«Obviously, I said yes!»
test: «Quite obviously, the door was locked.» What does obviously mean here? | Kindly, with a smile | Of course it was open | Quietly and gently | *Clearly, anyone could see it
exam: «Obviously not!» said Ben, with a sigh. What does obviously not mean here? | *Of course not, as anyone would know | Maybe not, so we should check first | Slowly and sadly | Clearly he was lost
